  "response": "The most likely cause is `src/billing.ts` reading the wall clock seven times (`Date.now()`/`new Date()` at lines 57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, and 273).\n\nA billing operation can therefore observe different dates if it runs across midnight, a billing-period boundary, or a fake-timer advancement. This is especially risky inside the 204-line `generateInvoice`: separate calculations may use subtly different “current” instants.\n\nProposed fix:\n\n```ts\nexport interface Clock {\n  now(): Date;\n}\n\nconst systemClock: Clock = {\n  now: () => new Date(),\n};\n\nexport function generateInvoice(\n  input: InvoiceInput,\n  clock: Clock = systemClock,\n): Invoice {\n  const now = clock.now(); // Capture exactly once.\n  // Pass `now` to all date-dependent helpers.\n}\n```\n\nThen:\n\n- Replace every internal `Date.now()` and `new Date()` with the captured `now`.\n- Use UTC consistently for billing-period comparisons and keys.\n- If `src/date.ts` participates, fix its mixed UTC/local methods and timezone-less parsing too; both can shift a value across a date boundary depending on the host timezone.\n- Add tests using a fixed clock immediately before and after midnight/month-end, ideally under two different timezones.\n- Strengthen `billing.test.ts`: one test has no assertions and another only a weak assertion, so the current suite may not reliably protect invoice dates, totals, and period boundaries.\n\nThe minimal safe change is clock injection plus one timestamp per invoice operation. Splitting `generateInvoice` into pure helpers would make the boundary rules easier to test, but it is secondary to removing the uncontrolled clock reads.",
